  "markdown": "# Biquad Filter Design\n\n## Overview\n\nBiquad (biquadratic) filters are second-order IIR filters with transfer function:\n\n```\nH(z) = (b0 + b1*z^-1 + b2*z^-2) / (1 + a1*z^-1 + a2*z^-2)\n```\n\nThey're the building blocks for most audio filters due to:\n- Numerical stability\n- Efficiency (5 multiplications per sample)\n- Cascade/parallel composition\n\n## Direct Form II Implementation\n\n```cpp\nclass BiquadFilter\n{\npublic:\n    BiquadFilter() : b0(1.0f), b1(0.0f), b2(0.0f),\n                     a1(0.0f), a2(0.0f),\n                     s1(0.0f), s2(0.0f) {}\n    \n    float process(float input)\n    {\n        float output = b0 * input + s1;\n        s1 = b1 * input - a1 * output + s2;\n        s2 = b2 * input - a2 * output;\n        return output;\n    }\n    \n    void setCoefficients(float b0_, float b1_, float b2_,\n                         float a1_, float a2_)\n    {\n        b0 = b0_; b1 = b1_; b2 = b2_;\n        a1 = a1_; a2 = a2_;\n    }\n    \n    void reset()\n    {\n        s1 = s2 = 0.0f;\n    }\n    \nprivate:\n    float b0, b1, b2, a1, a2;  // Coefficients\n    float s1, s2;               // State (Direct Form II)\n};\n```\n\n## Filter Types\n\n### Low-Pass Filter\n```cpp\nvoid setLowpass(float cutoff, float Q, float sampleRate)\n{\n    float omega = 2.0f * M_PI * cutoff / sampleRate;\n    float sinOmega = std::sin(omega);\n    float cosOmega = std::cos(omega);\n    float alpha = sinOmega / (2.0f * Q);\n    \n    float scale = 1.0f / (1.0f + alpha);\n    \n    b0 = (1.0f - cosOmega) * 0.5f * scale;\n    b1 = (1.0f - cosOmega) * scale;\n    b2 = b0;\n    a1 = -2.0f * cosOmega * scale;\n    a2 = (1.0f - alpha) * scale;\n}\n```\n\n### High-Pass Filter\n```cpp\nvoid setHighpass(float cutoff, float Q, float sampleRate)\n{\n    float omega = 2.0f * M_PI * cutoff / sampleRate;\n    float sinOmega = std::sin(omega);\n    float cosOmega = std::cos(omega);\n    float alpha = sinOmega / (2.0f * Q);\n    \n    float scale = 1.0f / (1.0f + alpha);\n    \n    b0 = (1.0f + cosOmega) * 0.5f * scale;\n    b1 = -(1.0f + cosOmega) * scale;\n    b2 = b0;\n    a1 = -2.0f * cosOmega * scale;\n    a2 = (1.0f - alpha) * scale;\n}\n```\n\n### Band-Pass Filter\n```cpp\nvoid setBandpass(float cutoff, float Q, float sampleRate)\n{\n    float omega = 2.0f * M_PI * cutoff / sampleRate;\n    float sinOmega = std::sin(omega);\n    float cosOmega = std::cos(omega);\n    float alpha = sinOmega / (2.0f * Q);\n    \n    float scale = 1.0f / (1.0f + alpha);\n    \n    b0 = alpha * scale;\n    b1 = 0.0f;\n    b2 = -alpha * scale;\n    a1 = -2.0f * cosOmega * scale;\n    a2 = (1.0f - alpha) * scale;\n}\n```\n\n### Peaking/Notch Filter\n```cpp\nvoid setPeaking(float cutoff, float Q, float gainDB, float sampleRate)\n{\n    float omega = 2.0f * M_PI * cutoff / sampleRate;\n    float sinOmega = std::sin(omega);\n    float cosOmega = std::cos(omega);\n    \n    float A = std::pow(10.0f, gainDB / 40.0f);\n    float alpha = sinOmega / (2.0f * Q);\n    \n    float scale = 1.0f / (1.0f + alpha / A);\n    \n    b0 = (1.0f + alpha * A) * scale;\n    b1 = -2.0f * cosOmega * scale;\n    b2 = (1.0f - alpha * A) * scale;\n    a1 = -2.0f * cosOmega * scale;\n    a2 = (1.0f - alpha / A) * scale;\n}\n```\n\n## JUCE Implementation\n\n```cpp\n// Using JUCE's DSP module\n#include <juce_dsp/juce_dsp.h>\n\nusing Filter = juce::dsp::IIR::Filter<float>;\nusing Coefficients = juce::dsp::IIR::Coefficients<float>;\n\nclass PluginProcessor\n{\npublic:\n    void prepare(double sampleRate, int maxBlockSize)\n    {\n        juce::dsp::ProcessSpec spec;\n        spec.sampleRate = sampleRate;\n        spec.maximumBlockSize = maxBlockSize;\n        spec.numChannels = 2;\n        \n        filter.prepare(spec);\n    }\n    \n    void processBlock(juce::AudioBuffer<float>& buffer)\n    {\n        juce::dsp::AudioBlock<float> block(buffer);\n        juce::dsp::ProcessContextReplacing<float> context(block);\n        filter.process(context);\n    }\n    \n    void updateFilter(float cutoff, float Q)\n    {\n        *filter.state = *Coefficients::makeLowPass(sampleRate, cutoff, Q);\n    }\n    \nprivate:\n    juce::dsp::ProcessorDuplicator<Filter, Coefficients> filter;\n    double sampleRate = 44100.0;\n};\n```\n\n## Cascade for Higher Orders\n\n```cpp\n// 4th-order filter using two cascaded biquads\nclass CascadedFilter\n{\npublic:\n    void setLowpass(float cutoff, float Q, float sampleRate)\n    {\n        // First section: Q = 0.5412 (Butterworth)\n        float Q1 = Q * 0.5412f;\n        section1.setLowpass(cutoff, Q1, sampleRate);\n        \n        // Second section: Q = 1.3066 (Butterworth)\n        float Q2 = Q * 1.3066f;\n        section2.setLowpass(cutoff, Q2, sampleRate);\n    }\n    \n    float process(float input)\n    {\n        return section2.process(section1.process(input));\n    }\n    \nprivate:\n    BiquadFilter section1, section2;\n};\n```\n\n## Butterworth Coefficients\n\nFor N-th order Butterworth filter:\n\n```cpp\n// Generate Butterworth Q values for cascade\nstd::vector<float> getButterworthQs(int order)\n{\n    std::vector<float> Qs;\n    int sections = order / 2;\n    \n    for (int k = 1; k <= sections; ++k)\n    {\n        float Q = 1.0f / (2.0f * std::sin(M_PI * (2.0f * k - 1.0f) / (2.0f * order)));\n        Qs.push_back(Q);\n    }\n    \n    return Qs;\n}\n```\n\n## Stability Considerations\n\n1. **Coefficient limits**: Keep |a1| < 2 and |a2| < 1\n2. **Denormal handling**: Add tiny offset to prevent denormals\n```cpp\n    void process(float input)\n{\n    const float denormal = 1e-15f;\n    float output = b0 * input + s1;\n    s1 = b1 * input - a1 * output + s2 + denormal;\n    s2 = b2 * input - a2 * output;\n    return output;\n}\n```\n\n3. **Precision**: For low frequencies, use double precision\n4. **Modulation**: Smooth coefficient changes to avoid clicks\n\n## Performance Tips\n\n| Technique | Benefit |\n|-----------|--------|\n| SIMD | Process 4 channels at once |\n| Inlining | Avoid function call overhead |\n| State arrays | Better cache locality |\n| Lookup tables | For common Q values |\n\n## References\n\n- Robert Bristow-Johnson's Audio EQ Cookbook\n- JUCE DSP Module documentation\n- \"Introduction to Digital Filters\" by Julius O. Smith",
